          "llm": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "confidence": "medium",
            "summary": "The skill's files and runtime instructions broadly align with a multi-agent dispatcher, but the installation instructions and scripts modify global OpenClaw configuration (openclaw.json), create persistent agent workspaces, set permissive subagent rules, and restart the Gateway — actions that are high‑impact and should be reviewed before running.",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "detail": "Name/description match the content: assets, templates, agent roles, DAG templates, and orchestration scripts are present and consistent with a multi-agent dispatcher. However, the skill asks to write global OpenClaw configuration (agents.list / openclaw.json), add six subagents and set main.subagents.allowAgents: [\"*\"], which is powerful — plausible for a dispatcher but worth flagging because it grants broad inter-agent permissions.",
                "label": "Purpose & Capability",
                "name": "purpose_capability",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "SKILL.md mandates running scripts/setup.sh as a required first step and explicitly requires the agent to perform environment detection and install steps before answering user requests. The instructions tell the agent to create/modify ~/.openclaw directories, copy template agent workspaces into ~/.openclaw/agents, write ~/.openclaw/configs/team-dispatch.json, edit openclaw.json (agents.list) and restart the Gateway. These are invasive, persistent system changes and the SKILL.md gives the agent broad discretion during setup.",
                "label": "Instruction Scope",
                "name": "instruction_scope",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "There is no remote download/install spec — this is an instruction-only skill with local scripts and assets. That reduces supply-chain risk (no external URL or package fetch documented). Risk remains because scripts are run locally and will write/modify user files, but there is no evidence of arbitrary remote code fetch in the provided manifest.",
                "label": "Install Mechanism",
                "name": "install_mechanism",
                "rating": "ok"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The skill requires no environment variables or external credentials, yet it modifies global agent configuration and filesystem locations under the user home (~/work, ~/.openclaw/*). It also writes notification configuration that can include Telegram chat IDs (disabled by default). The lack of declared credentials is consistent with the content, but the degree of filesystem/config writes (and the permissive 'allowAgents:[\"*\"]' setting) is disproportionate in the sense of broad privileges vs. a simple helper.",
                "label": "Credentials",
                "name": "environment_proportionality",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The skill creates persistent files and directories (agent workspaces, sessions, templates, configs), installs a low-frequency watcher, and writes into openclaw.json to add/modify agents and subagent allowances. While always:false and autonomous invocation are normal, modifying global agent list and restarting the Gateway are significant privileges — this skill will persistently alter the agent environment and grant broad subagent permissions.",
                "label": "Persistence & Privilege",
                "name": "persistence_privilege",
                "rating": "concern"
              }
            ],
            "guidance": "This package appears to be a genuine multi‑agent dispatcher, but it performs high‑impact, persistent changes to your OpenClaw environment. Before installing or running setup.sh: 1) Inspect scripts/setup.sh, scripts/watch.sh, and any script that edits openclaw.json to see exactly what files/keys they change and whether they call external endpoints. 2) Backup your existing ~/.openclaw/openclaw.json and related configs (or test inside a disposable account/container). 3) Pay special attention to the code path that sets main.subagents.allowAgents: [\"*\"] — consider restricting it instead of allowing wildcards. 4) Check for any network/notification code (Telegram/webhooks) and verify chat IDs / endpoints before enabling. 5) If you lack time to audit, run the skill in a sandbox VM/container so its filesystem and gateway restarts cannot affect your primary environment. If you want, provide the full contents of scripts/setup.sh and watch.py and I can point to the exact lines that change configs or perform network actions.",
